window.FIELD_CHAPTER_PARTS["book-3-full-book"][110] = "<h1>Glossary</h1>\n<p>236 terms. Each term is defined once, here and in its owning chapter; chapters link to it and may extend the idea but never redefine it.</p>\n<p><strong>Terms by owning chapter</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>C01</strong> The APM Role and How This Book Works: <a href=\"#gl-decision-rights\">decision rights</a>, <a href=\"#gl-product-triad\">product triad</a>, <a href=\"#gl-rotational-program\">rotational program</a></li>\n<li><strong>C02</strong> Product Thinking and Decision Foundations: <a href=\"#gl-cost-of-delay\">cost of delay</a>, <a href=\"#gl-first-principles-reasoning\">first-principles reasoning</a>, <a href=\"#gl-inversion\">inversion</a>, <a href=\"#gl-local-optimum\">local optimum</a>, <a href=\"#gl-pre-mortem\">pre-mortem</a>, <a href=\"#gl-product-thinking\">product thinking</a>, <a href=\"#gl-reversible-decision\">reversible decision</a>, <a href=\"#gl-second-order-effect\">second-order effect</a>, <a href=\"#gl-sunk-cost\">sunk cost</a>, <a href=\"#gl-tradeoff\">tradeoff</a></li>\n<li><strong>C03</strong> Structured Problem Solving: MECE, Issue Trees, Hypotheses: <a href=\"#gl-five-whys\">five whys</a>, <a href=\"#gl-framework-theater\">framework theater</a>, <a href=\"#gl-hypothesis\">hypothesis</a>, <a href=\"#gl-issue-tree\">issue tree</a>, <a href=\"#gl-mece\">MECE</a>, <a href=\"#gl-pareto-principle\">Pareto principle</a>, <a href=\"#gl-root-cause\">root cause</a>, <a href=\"#gl-symptom\">symptom</a></li>\n<li><strong>C04</strong> Estimation Under Pressure: <a href=\"#gl-anchor\">anchor</a>, <a href=\"#gl-estimation\">estimation</a>, <a href=\"#gl-order-of-magnitude\">order of magnitude</a>, <a href=\"#gl-sanity-check\">sanity check</a>, <a href=\"#gl-sensitivity-analysis\">sensitivity analysis</a></li>\n<li><strong>C05</strong> Metrics: Choosing, Defining and Reading Them: <a href=\"#gl-activation\">activation</a>, <a href=\"#gl-churn\">churn</a>, <a href=\"#gl-cohort\">cohort</a>, <a href=\"#gl-conversion-rate\">conversion rate</a>, <a href=\"#gl-counter-metric\">counter metric</a>, <a href=\"#gl-daily-and-monthly-active-users\">daily and monthly active users</a>, <a href=\"#gl-funnel\">funnel</a>, <a href=\"#gl-goodharts-law\">Goodhart's law</a>, <a href=\"#gl-guardrail-metric\">guardrail metric</a>, <a href=\"#gl-input-metric\">input metric</a>, <a href=\"#gl-instrumentation\">instrumentation</a>, <a href=\"#gl-lagging-indicator\">lagging indicator</a>, <a href=\"#gl-leading-indicator\">leading indicator</a>, <a href=\"#gl-metric\">metric</a>, <a href=\"#gl-metric-tree\">metric tree</a>, <a href=\"#gl-north-star-metric\">north star metric</a>, <a href=\"#gl-proxy-metric\">proxy metric</a>, <a href=\"#gl-retention\">retention</a></li>\n<li><strong>C06</strong> Users, Problems and Discovery: <a href=\"#gl-jobs-to-be-done\">jobs to be done</a>, <a href=\"#gl-persona\">persona</a>, <a href=\"#gl-problem-statement\">problem statement</a>, <a href=\"#gl-say-do-gap\">say-do gap</a>, <a href=\"#gl-user-segment\">user segment</a></li>\n<li><strong>C07</strong> Product Design: Designing for a User: <a href=\"#gl-pain-point\">pain point</a>, <a href=\"#gl-product-sense\">product sense</a></li>\n<li><strong>C08</strong> Improving an Existing Product: <a href=\"#gl-cannibalization\">cannibalization</a>, <a href=\"#gl-headroom\">headroom</a>, <a href=\"#gl-legacy-user\">legacy user</a></li>\n<li><strong>C09</strong> New Markets and Underserved Users: <a href=\"#gl-localization\">localization</a>, <a href=\"#gl-trust-signal\">trust signal</a>, <a href=\"#gl-underserved-user\">underserved user</a></li>\n<li><strong>C10</strong> UX and Product Quality: <a href=\"#gl-accessibility\">accessibility</a>, <a href=\"#gl-cognitive-load\">cognitive load</a>, <a href=\"#gl-dark-pattern\">dark pattern</a>, <a href=\"#gl-information-architecture\">information architecture</a>, <a href=\"#gl-nudge\">nudge</a>, <a href=\"#gl-usability-heuristic\">usability heuristic</a></li>\n<li><strong>C11</strong> Product Critique and Teardown Method: <a href=\"#gl-first-run-experience\">first-run experience</a>, <a href=\"#gl-teardown\">teardown</a></li>\n<li><strong>C12</strong> Statistics for PMs: <a href=\"#gl-base-rate\">base rate</a>, <a href=\"#gl-confidence-interval\">confidence interval</a>, <a href=\"#gl-effect-size\">effect size</a>, <a href=\"#gl-multiple-comparisons\">multiple comparisons</a>, <a href=\"#gl-p-value\">p-value</a>, <a href=\"#gl-percentile\">percentile</a>, <a href=\"#gl-practical-significance\">practical significance</a>, <a href=\"#gl-regression-to-the-mean\">regression to the mean</a>, <a href=\"#gl-simpsons-paradox\">Simpson's paradox</a>, <a href=\"#gl-standard-error\">standard error</a>, <a href=\"#gl-statistical-power\">statistical power</a>, <a href=\"#gl-survivorship-bias\">survivorship bias</a></li>\n<li><strong>C13</strong> Diagnosing a Metric Change: <a href=\"#gl-baseline\">baseline</a>, <a href=\"#gl-grain\">grain</a>, <a href=\"#gl-metric-diagnosis\">metric diagnosis</a>, <a href=\"#gl-mix-shift\">mix shift</a>, <a href=\"#gl-pull-forward\">pull-forward</a>, <a href=\"#gl-seasonality\">seasonality</a>, <a href=\"#gl-sql\">SQL</a></li>\n<li><strong>C14</strong> Experiment Design and Causal Reasoning: <a href=\"#gl-a-b-test\">A/B test</a>, <a href=\"#gl-causal-effect\">causal effect</a>, <a href=\"#gl-confounder\">confounder</a>, <a href=\"#gl-counterfactual\">counterfactual</a>, <a href=\"#gl-difference-in-differences\">difference-in-differences</a>, <a href=\"#gl-experiment\">experiment</a>, <a href=\"#gl-holdout\">holdout</a>, <a href=\"#gl-interference\">interference</a>, <a href=\"#gl-minimum-detectable-effect\">minimum detectable effect</a>, <a href=\"#gl-novelty-effect\">novelty effect</a>, <a href=\"#gl-peeking\">peeking</a>, <a href=\"#gl-randomization\">randomization</a>, <a href=\"#gl-sample-ratio-mismatch\">sample ratio mismatch</a></li>\n<li><strong>C15</strong> Prioritization and Tradeoffs: <a href=\"#gl-assumption\">assumption</a>, <a href=\"#gl-mvp\">minimum viable product</a>, <a href=\"#gl-opportunity-cost\">opportunity cost</a>, <a href=\"#gl-prioritization\">prioritization</a>, <a href=\"#gl-scope-creep\">scope creep</a>, <a href=\"#gl-time-box\">time-box</a></li>\n<li><strong>C16</strong> Specs, Engineering and Design Partnership: <a href=\"#gl-acceptance-criteria\">acceptance criteria</a>, <a href=\"#gl-backlog\">backlog</a>, <a href=\"#gl-definition-of-done\">definition of done</a>, <a href=\"#gl-non-goal\">non-goal</a>, <a href=\"#gl-prd\">product requirements document</a>, <a href=\"#gl-sprint\">sprint</a>, <a href=\"#gl-technical-debt\">technical debt</a>, <a href=\"#gl-user-story\">user story</a></li>\n<li><strong>C17</strong> Launch, Rollout and Execution Decisions: <a href=\"#gl-blast-radius\">blast radius</a>, <a href=\"#gl-canary-release\">canary release</a>, <a href=\"#gl-feature-flag\">feature flag</a>, <a href=\"#gl-postmortem\">postmortem</a>, <a href=\"#gl-rollback\">rollback</a>, <a href=\"#gl-rollback-trigger\">rollback trigger</a>, <a href=\"#gl-staged-rollout\">staged rollout</a></li>\n<li><strong>C18</strong> Strategy at APM Depth: <a href=\"#gl-contribution-margin\">contribution margin</a>, <a href=\"#gl-moat\">moat</a>, <a href=\"#gl-network-effect\">network effect</a>, <a href=\"#gl-okr\">OKR</a>, <a href=\"#gl-positioning\">positioning</a>, <a href=\"#gl-roadmap\">roadmap</a>, <a href=\"#gl-strategy\">strategy</a>, <a href=\"#gl-switching-cost\">switching cost</a>, <a href=\"#gl-total-addressable-market\">total addressable market</a>, <a href=\"#gl-unit-economics\">unit economics</a></li>\n<li><strong>C19</strong> Platforms and Ecosystems: <a href=\"#gl-cold-start-problem\">cold-start problem</a>, <a href=\"#gl-developer-experience\">developer experience</a>, <a href=\"#gl-disintermediation\">disintermediation</a>, <a href=\"#gl-multi-homing\">multi-homing</a>, <a href=\"#gl-platform\">platform</a>, <a href=\"#gl-take-rate\">take rate</a>, <a href=\"#gl-two-sided-market\">two-sided market</a></li>\n<li><strong>C20</strong> Go-to-Market, Pricing and Growth Basics: <a href=\"#gl-customer-acquisition-cost\">customer acquisition cost</a>, <a href=\"#gl-go-to-market\">go-to-market</a>, <a href=\"#gl-growth-loop\">growth loop</a>, <a href=\"#gl-lifetime-value\">lifetime value</a>, <a href=\"#gl-packaging\">packaging</a>, <a href=\"#gl-payback-period\">payback period</a>, <a href=\"#gl-product-led-growth\">product-led growth</a>, <a href=\"#gl-value-metric\">value metric</a>, <a href=\"#gl-willingness-to-pay\">willingness to pay</a></li>\n<li><strong>C21</strong> How Products Work: Technical Fluency: <a href=\"#gl-api\">API</a>, <a href=\"#gl-availability\">availability</a>, <a href=\"#gl-cache\">cache</a>, <a href=\"#gl-data-pipeline\">data pipeline</a>, <a href=\"#gl-database-index\">database index</a>, <a href=\"#gl-idempotency\">idempotency</a>, <a href=\"#gl-latency\">latency</a>, <a href=\"#gl-message-queue\">message queue</a>, <a href=\"#gl-on-device-processing\">on-device processing</a>, <a href=\"#gl-schema\">schema</a>, <a href=\"#gl-service-level-objective\">service level objective</a>, <a href=\"#gl-throughput\">throughput</a></li>\n<li><strong>C22</strong> PM-Level System Design: <a href=\"#gl-abuse-case\">abuse case</a>, <a href=\"#gl-eventual-consistency\">eventual consistency</a>, <a href=\"#gl-fan-out\">fan-out</a>, <a href=\"#gl-graceful-degradation\">graceful degradation</a>, <a href=\"#gl-rate-limiting\">rate limiting</a>, <a href=\"#gl-webhook\">webhook</a></li>\n<li><strong>C23</strong> Machine Learning, Ranking and Recommendation for PMs: <a href=\"#gl-candidate-generation\">candidate generation</a>, <a href=\"#gl-embedding\">embedding</a>, <a href=\"#gl-exploration\">exploration</a>, <a href=\"#gl-feedback-loop\">feedback loop</a>, <a href=\"#gl-label\">label</a>, <a href=\"#gl-model\">model</a>, <a href=\"#gl-offline-evaluation\">offline evaluation</a>, <a href=\"#gl-overfitting\">overfitting</a>, <a href=\"#gl-popularity-bias\">popularity bias</a>, <a href=\"#gl-position-bias\">position bias</a>, <a href=\"#gl-precision-recall\">precision and recall</a>, <a href=\"#gl-ranking\">ranking</a>, <a href=\"#gl-recommender-system\">recommender system</a>, <a href=\"#gl-threshold\">threshold</a>, <a href=\"#gl-training-data\">training data</a></li>\n<li><strong>C24</strong> AI Product Management: Foundations: <a href=\"#gl-agent\">agent</a>, <a href=\"#gl-context-window\">context window</a>, <a href=\"#gl-fine-tuning\">fine-tuning</a>, <a href=\"#gl-grounding\">grounding</a>, <a href=\"#gl-hallucination\">hallucination</a>, <a href=\"#gl-human-in-the-loop\">human in the loop</a>, <a href=\"#gl-llm\">large language model</a>, <a href=\"#gl-prompt\">prompt</a>, <a href=\"#gl-prompt-injection\">prompt injection</a>, <a href=\"#gl-rag\">retrieval-augmented generation</a>, <a href=\"#gl-token\">token</a></li>\n<li><strong>C25</strong> AI Product Management: Building, Evaluating and Governing: <a href=\"#gl-drift\">drift</a>, <a href=\"#gl-eval\">eval</a>, <a href=\"#gl-guardrail\">guardrail</a>, <a href=\"#gl-inter-rater-agreement\">inter-rater agreement</a>, <a href=\"#gl-model-card\">model card</a>, <a href=\"#gl-model-graded-eval\">model-graded eval</a>, <a href=\"#gl-red-teaming\">red-teaming</a>, <a href=\"#gl-regression-test\">regression test</a>, <a href=\"#gl-risk-tier\">risk tier</a>, <a href=\"#gl-shadow-mode\">shadow mode</a></li>\n<li><strong>C26</strong> 0→1 and Idea Evaluation: <a href=\"#gl-concierge-test\">concierge test</a>, <a href=\"#gl-kill-criteria\">kill criteria</a>, <a href=\"#gl-pivot\">pivot</a>, <a href=\"#gl-product-market-fit\">product-market fit</a>, <a href=\"#gl-riskiest-assumption\">riskiest assumption</a>, <a href=\"#gl-smoke-test\">smoke test</a></li>\n<li><strong>C27</strong> Domain Primers: <a href=\"#gl-ad-auction\">ad auction</a>, <a href=\"#gl-chargeback\">chargeback</a>, <a href=\"#gl-internal-platform\">internal platform</a>, <a href=\"#gl-know-your-customer\">know your customer</a>, <a href=\"#gl-liquidity\">liquidity</a>, <a href=\"#gl-net-revenue-retention\">net revenue retention</a>, <a href=\"#gl-prevalence\">prevalence</a>, <a href=\"#gl-sales-cycle\">sales cycle</a>, <a href=\"#gl-trust-and-safety\">trust and safety</a></li>\n<li><strong>C29</strong> Anti-Patterns: <a href=\"#gl-anti-pattern\">anti-pattern</a>, <a href=\"#gl-feature-factory\">feature factory</a>, <a href=\"#gl-hippo\">HiPPO</a>, <a href=\"#gl-vanity-metric\">vanity metric</a></li>\n<li><strong>C31</strong> Behavioral Interviews: Stories That Hold Up: <a href=\"#gl-signal\">signal</a>, <a href=\"#gl-story-bank\">story bank</a></li>\n<li><strong>C32</strong> Communication and Influence at APM Level: <a href=\"#gl-bluf\">bottom line up front</a>, <a href=\"#gl-decision-document\">decision document</a>, <a href=\"#gl-escalation\">escalation</a>, <a href=\"#gl-influence-without-authority\">influence without authority</a>, <a href=\"#gl-pyramid-principle\">pyramid principle</a>, <a href=\"#gl-stakeholder\">stakeholder</a></li>\n<li><strong>C33</strong> Interview Loops and Company Lenses: <a href=\"#gl-bar-raiser\">bar raiser</a>, <a href=\"#gl-company-brief\">company brief</a>, <a href=\"#gl-hiring-committee\">hiring committee</a>, <a href=\"#gl-interview-loop\">interview loop</a></li>\n<li><strong>C34</strong> Mock Interviews, Scoring and Calibration: <a href=\"#gl-calibration\">calibration</a>, <a href=\"#gl-deliberate-practice\">deliberate practice</a>, <a href=\"#gl-error-log\">error log</a>, <a href=\"#gl-mock-interview\">mock interview</a>, <a href=\"#gl-scorecard\">scorecard</a></li>\n<li><strong>C37</strong> The 12-Month Plan, Readiness Checklist and Your First 90 Days: <a href=\"#gl-readiness-gate\">readiness gate</a>, <a href=\"#gl-reading-map\">reading map</a></li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-entries\"></span>\n<h2>Entries</h2>\n<span id=\"gl-a-b-test\"></span>\n<h3>A/B test</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> An experiment in which users are randomly assigned to the current version (A) or a changed version (B) and the same metric is compared between the two groups.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> Half of new visitors see the current checkout and half see the new one; the share who buy is compared.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> Comparing sign-ups before and after a launch.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C14</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-abuse-case\"></span>\n<h3>abuse case</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> A way a system could be used to harm someone, analyzed like a requirement: who might misuse it, how, the harm, and the control that reduces it.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> A small tracker slipped into a bag to follow a person, with an alert to the person being followed as the control.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> An ordinary bug that affects only the person who triggered it.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C22</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-acceptance-criteria\"></span>\n<h3>acceptance criteria</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> The specific, testable conditions a story must meet to count as done, often written as given a situation, when an action, then an observable result.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> Given a list shared as view only, when a visitor opens the link, then they see the items and cannot change them.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> 'It should work well.'</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C16</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-accessibility\"></span>\n<h3>accessibility</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> The degree to which a product can be used by people with the widest range of abilities, including through assistive technology.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> Labels that a screen reader can speak, text with enough contrast and controls operable by keyboard or voice.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> An accessibility statement on a website with no accessible controls.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C10</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-activation\"></span>\n<h3>activation</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> The point at which a new user first experiences the product's core value, defined by an observable action.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> Sending a first message to a teammate.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> Creating an account.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C05</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-ad-auction\"></span>\n<h3>ad auction</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> The mechanism by which an advertising platform decides, each time a slot is available, which ad to show and at what price, typically ranking advertisers by their bid combined with the predicted chance the user responds and charging the winner the minimum needed to keep its position.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> A $2.00 bid with a 1.0% predicted click rate beats a $3.00 bid with 0.5% and pays $1.80 a click.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> Showing the ad with the highest bid and charging that bid.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C27</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-agent\"></span>\n<h3>agent</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> A system that uses a model to plan and take actions through tools in pursuit of a goal.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> An assistant that reads a calendar, proposes times, and sends invitations.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> A model that answers one question and stops.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C24</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-anchor\"></span>\n<h3>anchor</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> A well-known quantity used as the starting point for an estimate.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> The number of seconds in a day, or the population of a country.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> A number guessed with no basis and presented as fact.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C04</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-anti-pattern\"></span>\n<h3>anti-pattern</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> A common response to a recurring problem that looks sensible, is easy to adopt and reliably produces a worse result than the alternatives; the term names the response, not the person.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> Judging a team by the number of features shipped instead of the outcomes they change.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> A one-off mistake with no recurring cause.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C29</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-api\"></span>\n<h3>API</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> A defined way for one piece of software to request data or actions from another.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> A weather service returning a forecast when sent a city name.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> A website's visual design.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C21</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-assumption\"></span>\n<h3>assumption</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> A belief a plan depends on that has not yet been verified.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> &quot;Users will pay for faster delivery.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> A verified measurement.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C15</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-availability\"></span>\n<h3>availability</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> The share of time a service works as intended, usually stated in nines; availabilities of services in a chain multiply.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> 99.9% availability allows about 43.8 minutes of downtime in a month.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> The speed of a single response.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C21</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-backlog\"></span>\n<h3>backlog</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> The single list of work a team could do next, ranked by the product manager against the goal.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> A list of 40 items in priority order whose top ten are clear, estimated and testable.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> A pile of unranked requests from many people.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C16</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-bar-raiser\"></span>\n<h3>bar raiser</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> An interviewer from outside the hiring team, trained to hold a consistent hiring standard across teams, who in some processes can block a hire the team wants (as candidates report for one company).</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> An outside interviewer in the loop who probes stories for detail and writes a separate assessment.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> The hiring manager of the team that is recruiting.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C33</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-base-rate\"></span>\n<h3>base rate</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> How common something is in the population before any new evidence is considered.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> If 1 in 1,000 accounts is fraudulent, a flag must be judged against that rate.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> The accuracy of the detection model.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C12</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-baseline\"></span>\n<h3>baseline</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> The value a metric would be expected to take without the event under investigation, estimated from recent history and the same period in earlier cycles.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> This Tuesday's orders compared with the average of recent Tuesdays and with the same holiday week last year.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> Yesterday's value, which carries the day-of-week effect and one day's noise.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C13</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-blast-radius\"></span>\n<h3>blast radius</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> How many users or systems a failure would affect.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> A bug behind a flag at 1% exposure affects 10,000 of a million users; the same bug at 100% affects all of them.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> The probability that a failure occurs.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C17</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-bluf\"></span>\n<h3>bottom line up front</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> The practice of stating the conclusion or request first and the supporting detail after it.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> I recommend we delay the launch one week because the checkout bug affects 3% of orders; I need your approval by Thursday.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> Two minutes of background before the point.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C32</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-cache\"></span>\n<h3>cache</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> A stored copy of data kept close to where it is needed so it can be served faster than recomputing or refetching it, at the cost of possible staleness.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> Product details served from memory for a few minutes instead of querying the database each time.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> The system of record that holds the authoritative data.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C21</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-calibration\"></span>\n<h3>calibration</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> The alignment of your self-assessment with outside judgment, measured as bias (mean of your score minus the outside score) and error (mean absolute difference).</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> A candidate whose self-scores run 0.75 points above a partner's over eight mocks and who corrects for it.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> Feeling confident that you are ready.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C34</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-canary-release\"></span>\n<h3>canary release</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> A first release of a change to a small subset of users or servers that acts as an early warning before wider exposure.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> Showing the rewritten account page to 1% of users for a business day while watching errors.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> A release to all users with monitoring added afterward.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C17</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-candidate-generation\"></span>\n<h3>candidate generation</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> The cheap first stage of a recommender or search system that narrows a huge catalog to a manageable set of plausible items for the heavier ranking stage.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> Narrowing 100 million videos to about 1,000 candidates using similarity, follows and trending.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> The final ordering of the items shown.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C23</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-cannibalization\"></span>\n<h3>cannibalization</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> When a new feature or offer takes away use or revenue from an existing one of the same company, so that gross gains overstate net gains.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> A cheaper plan that mostly attracts existing full-price subscribers.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> A new plan whose subscribers are all people who were not customers before.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C08</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-causal-effect\"></span>\n<h3>causal effect</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> The difference in outcome caused by a change, compared with what would have happened without it.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> The extra purchases that occurred because of the new recommendation module.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> Purchases by users who used the module.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C14</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-chargeback\"></span>\n<h3>chargeback</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> A reversal of a card payment forced by the cardholder's bank after a dispute, usually with a fee, and a main cost of fraud.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> A $60 purchase reversed with a $15 fee.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> A refund the merchant chooses to give.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C27</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-churn\"></span>\n<h3>churn</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> The share of users or customers lost in a period, the complement of retention.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> 4% of subscribers cancelling in a month.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> The number of new subscribers added.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C05</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-cognitive-load\"></span>\n<h3>cognitive load</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> The mental effort a task demands of working memory; interfaces that add unneeded effort slow people and cause errors.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> A 22-toggle list with no grouping that makes people scan and remember.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> A short, grouped list with clear labels.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C10</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-cohort\"></span>\n<h3>cohort</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> A group of users who share a starting event or period, tracked together over time.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> Everyone who signed up in March.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> All users active today.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C05</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-cold-start-problem\"></span>\n<h3>cold-start problem</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> The difficulty a platform faces when its value depends on participants who will not join until the platform is already valuable.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> A marketplace where buyers will not come without sellers and sellers will not come without buyers.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> A mature platform with steady demand and supply.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C19</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-company-brief\"></span>\n<h3>company brief</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> A one-page summary of a company's products, users, strategy, metrics, competitors and recent moves, built from public sources, plus your own angle and questions, used to answer 'why us' and to ground product answers.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> A page with the main products, how the company makes money, two competitors, the last three launches, one critique with an improvement and three questions.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> A list of facts copied from the careers page with no view.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C33</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-concierge-test\"></span>\n<h3>concierge test</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> A test in which the service is delivered manually to a few customers, with little or no software, to learn whether it is wanted, how it should work and what it costs.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> Matching meal plans to 12 customers by hand for 12 weeks at $60 a week.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> A fully automated product launched to everyone.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C26</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-confidence-interval\"></span>\n<h3>confidence interval</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> A range of values consistent with the data at a stated confidence level, produced by a procedure that captures the true value at that rate over repeated samples.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> A lift of 1.2% with a 95% interval from 0.4% to 2.0%.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> A guarantee that the true value is inside this one range.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C12</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-confounder\"></span>\n<h3>confounder</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> A factor linked to both a change and an outcome that makes the observed relationship misleading.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> Heavy users adopt a feature and also retain better, so adoption appears to cause retention.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> A random event affecting all users equally.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C14</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-context-window\"></span>\n<h3>context window</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> The maximum amount of text, in tokens, that a model can consider at once, covering instructions, supplied documents, the conversation so far and the answer.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> A document too long to fit in the window must be split or retrieved in parts.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> The model's training data.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C24</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-contribution-margin\"></span>\n<h3>contribution margin</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> Revenue minus the variable costs of a unit (cost of goods, delivery, payment fees, support), before fixed costs.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> An $80 grocery order with $17.60 gross margin and $12 delivery cost contributes $5.60.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> Net profit after allocated overhead.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C18</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-conversion-rate\"></span>\n<h3>conversion rate</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> The share of a defined population that completes a defined action within a stated window.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> First purchases divided by new visitors within seven days.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> A bare statement that conversion is 3% with no population or window.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C05</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-cost-of-delay\"></span>\n<h3>cost of delay</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> The value lost for each unit of time that a decision or release is postponed.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> A fix that recovers 1% of weekly revenue loses that amount every week it waits.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> The time a task takes to complete.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C02</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-counter-metric\"></span>\n<h3>counter metric</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> A metric paired with a target specifically to expose the damage that optimizing the target could cause.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> Support tickets per order, tracked beside order volume.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> A second goal with its own target.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C05</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-counterfactual\"></span>\n<h3>counterfactual</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> What would have happened to the same units if they had not received the change.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> The retention of treated users had they never seen the feature.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> The retention of a different, unrelated group.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C14</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-customer-acquisition-cost\"></span>\n<h3>customer acquisition cost</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> The average cost to acquire one new customer: sales and marketing spend in a period divided by the new customers acquired in it. Abbreviated CAC.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> $45 per new subscriber including salaries, tools and promotions.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> Only the advertising spend divided by clicks.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C20</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-daily-and-monthly-active-users\"></span>\n<h3>daily and monthly active users</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> The counts of distinct users who did a defined qualifying action on one day (DAU) or in a 30-day window (MAU); DAU divided by MAU shows how often monthly users come back.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> 200,000 daily active users and 1,000,000 monthly active users: one in five monthly users shows up on a given day.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> Total registered accounts, which includes people who never return.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C05</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-dark-pattern\"></span>\n<h3>dark pattern</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> A user-interface design that tricks or pressures people into actions they did not intend, to the benefit of the business.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> A subscription that takes one click to join and many steps across several screens to cancel.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> A short retention step that offers a pause and keeps a one-click exit.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C10</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-data-pipeline\"></span>\n<h3>data pipeline</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> A set of automated steps that moves data from where it is produced to where it is analyzed, collecting, cleaning, transforming and loading it, in batches or as a stream.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> A nightly job that turns raw app events into the tables behind a dashboard.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> A one-off spreadsheet export.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C21</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-database-index\"></span>\n<h3>database index</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> A data structure that lets a database find rows by a column's value without scanning the whole table, at the cost of storage and some write time.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> An index on category and city that turns a 2.1-second query into 12 milliseconds.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> A full table scan.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C21</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-decision-document\"></span>\n<h3>decision document</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> A short written proposal that frames a decision for the people who must make it: the decision needed and deadline, context, options, a recommendation with reasons, what would change it, risks and next steps.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> A one-page memo asking for a choice of trial length by Friday with three options and a recommendation.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> A slide deck that presents findings with no ask.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C32</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-decision-rights\"></span>\n<h3>decision rights</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> The allocation of who makes which decisions on a team, and who must be consulted or informed; rarely written down, and unclear rights cause more conflict than disagreement about substance.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> The PM decides what to build and why; engineering decides how; design decides the interaction.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> A reporting line or job title.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C01</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-definition-of-done\"></span>\n<h3>definition of done</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> The shared checklist a piece of work must meet before it counts as complete, such as code reviewed, tests passing, accessibility checked, instrumented and documented.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> A story is done only when its acceptance tests pass, accessibility is checked and events are logged.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> Done meaning 'the developer says it works.'</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C16</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-deliberate-practice\"></span>\n<h3>deliberate practice</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> A form of practice, described in the research on expertise, in which you work on a specific aspect of performance just beyond your current ability, with immediate feedback and repetition.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> Ten metric-definition drills each checked against a success test and retested after three days.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> Logging hours of unscored, unfocused practice.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C34</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-developer-experience\"></span>\n<h3>developer experience</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> The sum of what it takes for a developer to discover, understand, integrate and succeed with a platform: documentation, examples, software kits, error messages, tooling, support and pricing.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> A first working request within an hour, guided by clear errors and a quick start that runs as written.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> A powerful API with outdated examples and errors that say only 'invalid request.'</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C19</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-difference-in-differences\"></span>\n<h3>difference-in-differences</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> An estimate of an effect as the change over time in a treated group minus the change over time in a comparison group, which removes shared trends under the assumption that the groups would have moved in parallel without the change.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> Conversion up 1.5 points in the treated region and 0.6 points in a comparison region gives an estimated effect of 0.9 points.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> The before-and-after change in the treated region alone.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C14</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-disintermediation\"></span>\n<h3>disintermediation</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> Participants bypassing the platform to transact directly after being matched, which reduces the platform's revenue and data.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> A customer and provider rebooking by phone after a first job found on a marketplace.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> Two users who never found each other through the platform.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C19</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-drift\"></span>\n<h3>drift</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> The change over time in inputs, user behavior or the world that makes a deployed model perform worse than when it was evaluated.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> A support assistant's accuracy falling after the company changes its policies.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> A one-time bug introduced by a release.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C25</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-effect-size\"></span>\n<h3>effect size</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> The size of a difference or relationship, stated in units that can be compared with a decision threshold (absolute points, relative percent, or standardized units).</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> A lift of 0.1 percentage points, which is 2% relative, worth 10,000 purchases a month at the traffic in question.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> A p-value, which says whether a difference can be told from zero and not how large it is.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C12</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-embedding\"></span>\n<h3>embedding</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> A list of numbers that represents an item, user or word so that similar things lie close together, letting a system find similar items quickly.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> Finding the 500 videos nearest to a viewer's recent watches.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> A plain category label.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C23</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-error-log\"></span>\n<h3>error log</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> A running list of the specific mistakes made in practice, each with its cause and the fix to apply, reviewed regularly so that recurring errors become the next drills.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> Spent 20 minutes on users and 3 on metrics; fix: state a clock aloud; retest in 72 hours.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> A vague note to manage time better.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C34</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-escalation\"></span>\n<h3>escalation</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> Taking a problem or disagreement to someone with more authority to resolve it; used well it is a way to get a decision, not a way to win.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> Raising a safety disagreement with the decision-maker openly, with the other person informed.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> Going around a colleague without telling them to win an argument.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C32</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-estimation\"></span>\n<h3>estimation</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> Producing a reasoned approximate number from stated assumptions and a decomposition of the quantity.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> Estimating daily rides in a city from population, share who ride, and rides per rider.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> Recalling a published statistic.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C04</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-eval\"></span>\n<h3>eval</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> A repeatable test set and scoring method used to measure the quality of an AI system's output.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> A set of 300 real support questions with reference answers and a grading rubric.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> Trying a few prompts by hand and judging by feel.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C25</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-eventual-consistency\"></span>\n<h3>eventual consistency</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> A property in which copies of data may differ for a short time but will agree if no new changes arrive.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> A like count that is a few seconds behind on one device.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> An account balance that must be the same everywhere at once.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C22</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-experiment\"></span>\n<h3>experiment</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> A controlled test in which units are randomly assigned to variants so the effect of a change can be measured.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> Half of users see the new checkout and half see the old.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> Comparing this month's sales with last month's.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C14</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-exploration\"></span>\n<h3>exploration</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> Deliberately showing items whose value the system is unsure of, to learn, at some cost to immediate engagement, as opposed to exploiting what it already believes is best.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> Reserving 8% of home-screen slots for unfamiliar songs.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> Always showing the highest-scoring items.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C23</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-fan-out\"></span>\n<h3>fan-out</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> One action causing many deliveries or updates, such as one post reaching a million followers; the main cost driver in feeds and notifications (distinct from the join fan-out in a query).</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> A post written into the feed lists of 200 followers.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> A one-to-one message.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C22</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-feature-factory\"></span>\n<h3>feature factory</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> A team that is rewarded for shipping features rather than for the outcomes the features change; signalled by low outcome-metric coverage on the roadmap and few shipped items reviewed afterward.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> Fourteen launches in a half-year with no one able to say which improved completion.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> A team that ships quickly and reviews each launch against a target.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C29</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-feature-flag\"></span>\n<h3>feature flag</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> A switch that turns functionality on or off for chosen users without shipping a new release.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> Enabling a redesigned page for 5% of users.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> A branch in version control.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C17</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-feedback-loop\"></span>\n<h3>feedback loop</h3>\n";
